[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.a6_0"
text = "Not necessarily. A network can be very confident and wrong, especially on photos unlike its training ones, such as a fox or a drawing. The percentage is how sure it is, not how often it is correct."

[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.kicker"
text = "Training"

[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.p1"
text = "The two scores can be any numbers, such as 2.1 and -0.4. To read them as chances, they are passed through softmax: each score is raised to the power of e, and the results are divided by their sum. The outputs are between 0 and 1 and add up to 1, so 0.93 means 93 percent."

[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.p4"
text = "Only the difference of the two scores matters. The Recognize tab shows these probabilities as two bars."

[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.q6_0"
text = "Does 99 percent mean it is right 99 times out of 100?"

[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.title"
text = "Scores and probabilities"

[[string]]
id = "help.softmax.try"
text = "Open the Recognize tab"

[[string]]
id = "help.split.a6_0"
text = "Each new training uses its own random split. A saved network remembers its split, so continuing to train it uses the same one."

[[string]]
id = "help.split.fig2"
text = "The training loss keeps falling, the validation loss turns up: the network begins to memorize."

[[string]]
id = "help.split.kicker"
text = "Data"

[[string]]
id = "help.split.note4"
text = "The split is random and does not look at breeds, so very similar photos of the same animal can land on both sides, which makes the validation numbers a little too hopeful."

[[string]]
id = "help.split.p1"
text = "To know whether the network really learned, it has to be tested on photos it has not learned from. So before training the dataset is split at random into 80 percent training photos and 20 percent validation photos, with the same share of each class in both. The network learns from the first part and is only graded on the second."

[[string]]
id = "help.split.p3"
text = "When the two numbers drift apart like that, the network is overfitting: it knows its photos by heart and does worse on new ones. More photos, augmentation and fewer epochs all push against it."

[[string]]
id = "help.split.q6_0"
text = "Why does the same dataset give a different split?"

[[string]]
id = "help.split.title"
text = "Training and validation photos"

[[string]]
id = "help.split.try"
text = "Open the Train tab"

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.a6_0"
text = "Not quite. It tells how much the filter contributed for this one photo. A filter that is silent here may be decisive for another photo."

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.kicker"
text = "Looking inside"

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.p1"
text = "For the photo on the Recognize tab, the program asks of every filter: how much did you push the answer toward the second class or toward the first? The vote of a filter is its answer times the gradient of the difference (second score minus first score) with respect to that answer: a filter that is active and whose growth would favor the second class votes for it."

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.p4"
text = "Under the cells, two bars show the vote of the picked filter: the bar of the animal it pushes toward grows with the size of its push, next to the strongest push of the layer. Without the biases the votes of a layer add up to exactly the difference of the scores; the biases make up the small rest."

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.q6_0"
text = "Is a vote the same as importance?"

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.title"
text = "Votes for a class"

[[string]]
id = "help.votes.try"
text = "Open the Filters tab"

